    ('harness_libssh_service_request_name_not_checked', 'named:harness_libssh_service_request_name_not_checked', 'HARNESS', 'SERVICE_REQUEST for a service other than ssh-userauth: the sshpuffin libssh harness callback (put.c cb_service_request) accepts any name, wolfSSH rejects it in the library; libssh then authenticates, wolfSSH never does. Harness configuration gap, not a library defect (Gate 0).', 3767),
    ('kex_not_finalised_by_libssh_client_mapper_peer_public_key', 'claim:client:L:mapper_peer_public_key', 'HARNESS', 'client role: GATE 0 -- APPARATUS, not a library divergence. The bucket NAME is kept so it still matches the on-disk corpus directory, but the name is misleading: libssh refuses nothing here. The recorded error mapper_peer_public_key is manufactured by ssh_rootcause.error_class() from the sshpuffin prefixes "error executing a function symbol" and "error evaluating a term" -- a term-evaluation failure in our own harness, not a libssh protocol error. Swept over all 26 libssh-attributed mapper_* buckets (498 sampled per-PUT logs): 498 apparatus, 0 native libssh errors. wolfSSH hits the SAME term-evaluation failure (597/597), so no stack tolerated it; and the input is canonical in about 70%% of sampled traces rather than fuzzed. The diff shape is uniform in 412/412 sampled: Claims.DifferentTypes(SshClaimInner) + Knowledges.DifferentTypes(AlignedTranscript, source: Decryption) -- our decryption/alignment layer produced an aligned transcript for one PUT and not the other. An earlier revision of this row read "libssh refuses a fuzzed KEX-phase input the other stack tolerated", which is false in all three of its clauses.', 3735),
    ('wolfssh_aead_reject_unexplained_server', 'aead:server:unexplained', 'AUDIT', "wolfSSH AES-GCM tag failure (gerr=-180) surviving every STRUCTURAL cause: key-schedule shape wolfSSH accepts elsewhere, canonical hash pattern, correct invocation counter, schedule sound on the face of the term, no plaintext injection, no stale pre-rekey schedule. This stays the FAIL-OPEN residue by design - no rule distinguishes it, so a new corpus landing traces here must re-measure. For the PRESENT population the cause IS established, by execution rather than by rule: pipeline/hcheck.py compared the attacker's computed exchange hash against the PUT's OWN session_id claim on every trace in this bucket and found a mismatch in 3,772/3,772 - the derived keys cannot match, so wolfSSH's rejection is correct and libssh processing it anyway is the usual CVE-2026-59847 illustration. Tag stays AUDIT because that is a property of these traces, not of the rule. CORRECTION (cold audit): 'no rule distinguishes them' was inaccurate -- no SOUND rule does. hash_pattern() abstracts each exchange-hash argument to its head function plus a query flag and discards the query indices, so two terms hashing DIFFERENT KEXINITs are identical under it; a rule comparing the hashed I_C against the KEXINIT actually sent fires on 79.5% of this bucket but also on 25.2% of traffic wolfSSH ACCEPTED, so it discriminates without being sound. A cold-audit claim that 103 of these carry nameable value-level anomalies (84 with V_C/V_S TRANSPOSED) does NOT survive checking and is withdrawn: that test was role-blind, and the 90 traces it flagged are exactly the 90 CLIENT-role residue traces, where v_c being the peer's (wolfSSH's) banner is canonical -- in a client-role trace the attacker IS the server. Role-aware re-measurement finds 0 transposed at term level and 2 at value level. Nothing meaningful can be split out today.", 3683),
