    ('known_wolfssh_client_sends_during_incomplete_rekey', 'named:known_wolfssh_client_sends_during_incomplete_rekey', 'RFC-LOW', 'wolfSSH CLIENT sends a non-transport message between its rekey KEXINIT and NEWKEYS (RFC 4253 s7.1 MUST NOT).', 1181),
    ('harness_wolfssh_fwd_cancel_skips_allowlist', 'named:harness_wolfssh_fwd_cancel_skips_allowlist', 'HARNESS', 'cancel-tcpip-forward: the sshpuffin wolfSSH harness fwdCb returns success on the cleanup path without the allow-list check its setup path applies; the libssh harness has no cancel branch (REQUEST_FAILURE). Harness defect (Gate 0).', 1123),
    ('wolfssh_aead_reject_stale_pre_rekey_schedule_server', 'aead:server:stale_pre_rekey_schedule', 'KNOWN-CVE', "The packet wolfSSH rejects is sealed under the PRE-REKEY key schedule: its key/IV term appears among the encrypted packets sent before the last NEWKEYS and not among those sent after. RFC 4253 s7.2/s7.3 - after NEWKEYS both sides MUST take the new keys into use, and those keys derive from the NEW exchange hash H'; only session_id keeps the first H. wolfSSH had rotated, the attacker had not, so the tag fails and wolfSSH is correct. NOTE: this is invisible to an 'attacker H == PUT session_id' check, because the stale H IS the session id - it is the one blind spot of that test. libssh does not reject it (CVE-2026-59847).", 1054),
    ('transcript_server_first_libssh_93_wolfssh_92', 'shape:server:L93/W92', 'BENIGN', 'server role, transcript-only divergence (no status or claim difference): at the first point where the two decrypted transcripts differ, libssh sent 93(CHANNEL_WINDOW_ADJUST) and wolfSSH 92(CHANNEL_OPEN_FAILURE). Connection-protocol reply presence/count/order/content latitude; authentication agrees (guarded).', 1050),
